window.docContents[126].resolve({"/#inductive-props":{"contents":" is an inductively-defined relation.\nJust as inductive can be used to create new datatypes, it can be used to create new propositions.\nWhen a proposition takes an argument, it is referred to as a predicate that may be true for some, but not all, potential arguments.\nPropositions that take multiple arguments are called relations.\n\nEach constructor of an inductively defined proposition is a way to prove it.\nIn other words, the declaration of the proposition describes the different forms of evidence that it is true.\nA proposition with no arguments that has a single constructor can be quite easy to prove:\n\n\n\nThe proof consists of using its constructor:\n\n\n\nIn fact, the proposition , which should always be easy to prove, is defined just like :\n\n\n\nInductively-defined propositions that don't take arguments are not nearly as interesting as inductively-defined datatypes.\nThis is because data is interesting in its own right—the natural number  is different from the number 35, and someone who has ordered 3 pizzas will be upset if 35 arrive at their door 30 minutes later.\nThe constructors of a proposition describe ways in which the proposition can be true, but once a proposition has been proved, there is no need to know which underlying constructors were used.\nThis is why most interesting inductively-defined types in the  universe take arguments.\n\nThe inductively-defined predicate  states that its argument is three:The mechanism used here is just like indexed families such as , except the resulting type is a proposition that can be proved rather than data that can be used.\n\nUsing this predicate, it is possible to prove that three is indeed three:\n\n\n\nSimilarly,  is a predicate that states that its argument is :\n\n\n\nIf a number is three, then the result of adding two to it should be five.\nThis can be expressed as a theorem statement:\n\n\n\nThe resulting goal has a function type:\n\n\n\nThus, the  tactic can be used to convert the argument into an assumption:\n\n\n\n\n\nGiven the assumption that  is three, it should be possible to use the constructor of  to complete the proof:\n\n\n\nHowever, this results in an error:\n\n\n\nThis error occurs because  is not definitionally equal to .\nIn an ordinary function definition, dependent pattern matching on the assumption  could be used to refine  to .\nThe tactic equivalent of dependent pattern matching is , which has a syntax similar to that of induction:\n\n\n\nIn the remaining case,  has been refined to :\n\n\n\nBecause  is definitionally equal to , the constructor is now applicable:\n\n\n\nThe standard false proposition  has no constructors, making it impossible to provide direct evidence for.\nThe only way to provide evidence for  is if an assumption is itself impossible, similarly to how nomatch can be used to mark code that the type system can see is unreachable.\nAs described in the initial Interlude on proofs, the negation  is short for .\n can also be written .\n\nIt is not the case that four is three:\n\n\n\nThe initial proof goal contains :\n\n\n\nThe fact that it's actually a function type can be exposed using :\n\n\n\n\n\nBecause the goal is a function type,  can be used to convert the argument into an assumption.\nThere is no need to keep , as  can unfold the definition of  itself:\n\n\n\n\n\nIn this proof, the  tactic solves the goal immediately:\n\n\n\nJust as a pattern match on a  doesn't need to include a case for , a proof by cases over  doesn't need to include a case for .\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Programming, Proving, and Performance\u0009Arrays and Termination\u0009Inequality","header":"8.3.1.1. Inductively-Defined Propositions, Predicates, and Relations","id":"/#inductive-props"}});